1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,240
In this tutorial, we'll provide a guided tour of the Burp Suite user interface and we'll

2
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:17,660
point out many of the cool tools, features and options that you need to know about to

3
00:00:17,660 --> 00:00:19,760
get full value from Burp Suite.

4
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:23,720
So when Burp first opens, you see the Project Launcher here.

5
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:28,560
This is where you can select what kind of project to create or open and the configuration

6
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:29,560
options to use.

7
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:34,440
We cover this in a different tutorial, so for now I'm just going to launch a default

8
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:37,160
new project.

9
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:42,920
In the main Burp UI, at the top we have a series of tabs containing different Burp tools and

10
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:43,920
options.

11
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:47,740
This is the quickest way to get into the different areas of Burp that you'll need to be working

12
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:48,740
with.

13
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:56,360
In the Dashboard tab, this is where you can view and configure Burp's automated tasks,

14
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:58,120
things like scans and live tasks.

15
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:03,000
You can also see other data that's generated as Burp runs.

16
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,600
To see that in action very quickly, I'm just going to run a very quick scan.

17
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:13,480
Kicking off that scan opens the task.

18
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:21,480
It creates this entry on the Dashboard and we can see details of the scan as it progresses.

19
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:28,360
So here we can see the requests that are being made and the numbers of issues that are being

20
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:29,360
reported.

21
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:35,320
Under Issue Activity, we can see a live list of all of the issues as they are discovered

22
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:36,360
from anywhere within Burp.

23
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:41,240
Under the event log, we can see a log of interesting events and other information that's occurred.

24
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:48,840
If we click on View Details on a task, this opens a window just for that task where we can see

25
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:50,840
more detailed information about it.

26
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:54,920
For a scan, we can see all of the items that are being audited.

27
00:01:54,920 --> 00:02:00,440
We can again see the Issue Activity log just for this scan and the event log just for this scan.

28
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:07,720
In the Target tab, this is where Burp records all of the information that has been found

29
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:10,920
against different websites that you browse or websites that you scan.

30
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:16,760
So on the left, we have a folder view of the URL structure on each site.

31
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:20,680
Then we can see the content that exists at different URLs.

32
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:23,320
And finally, we can see the issues that have been reported.

33
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:29,560
Under Target Scope, you can tell Burp exactly what websites you are targeting,

34
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:31,320
what's in scope and what's out of scope.

35
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:36,040
And you can use Target Scope to drive all kinds of cool features throughout Burp.

36
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:38,520
We'll cover Target Scope in a separate tutorial.

37
00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:46,600
The proxy is the tool that you use to intercept, view, and modify traffic between your browser

38
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:48,520
and the target server.

39
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:52,840
So just to very quickly see how that works, I'm going to open Burp's browser and visit a URL.

40
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:03,080
Here in the intercept view, we can forward, edit different requests that are happening and responses.

41
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:04,520
I'm just going to forward everything for now.

42
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:10,280
In the HDB history view, we can see all of the requests that have been made.

43
00:03:10,280 --> 00:03:12,200
Look at the requests, look at the responses.

44
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:16,360
We could do the same for WebSockets history if the application is using WebSockets.

45
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:22,040
And we could also, in the options tab, control all of the settings that are used to fine tune

46
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:23,480
the behavior of Burp proxy.

47
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:29,880
So one thing that's useful is anywhere you see a config panel in Burp or many other panels as well,

48
00:03:29,880 --> 00:03:31,320
you have these icons.

49
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:37,240
If we click on the question mark, we get some immediate contextual inline documentation for

50
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:38,520
the feature that we're looking at.

51
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:43,960
And if you click on the gear icon, this is where you can load or save the options just from this

52
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:46,680
panel, or you could just restore defaults if you need to.

53
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:54,360
Burp Intruder is a tool that is used for automating custom attacks.

54
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:59,240
So the way this works is you send a request into Intruder from elsewhere in Burp.

55
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:05,000
So if we go to the proxy history and find a suitable request, we can do send to Intruder.

56
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:07,960
This opens a new tab in Intruder just for this request.

57
00:04:07,960 --> 00:04:12,040
We can configure the target, details of where we want payloads to go,

58
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:14,280
and details of the payloads themselves.

59
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:18,040
We'll cover how to use Burp Intruder in a separate tutorial.

60
00:04:20,280 --> 00:04:26,040
Burp Repeater is used to reissue an individual request and modify it and send it again over and

61
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:26,280
over.

62
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:32,680
So if we do the same, if we send a request to Burp Repeater, here the request appears,

63
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:36,360
we can edit it, we can click send, and we can see the response.

64
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:39,880
Again, Burp Repeater is covered in a separate tutorial.

65
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:45,080
Moving along the top, we have various other tools you use more occasionally.

66
00:04:45,080 --> 00:04:48,520
So Burp Sequencer is used to analyse the randomness,

67
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:53,560
the entropy that is contained in session tokens or other data that needs to be random.

68
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:59,160
It runs various statistical tests on a big data sample to tell you if the data appears to be random.

69
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:04,440
Burp Decoder is used to decode and encode data in various formats.

70
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:09,160
So if you run into data that is URL encoded, HTML encoded, Base64 encoded,

71
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:10,200
you can work with it here.

72
00:05:12,280 --> 00:05:17,960
Burp Comparer is used to do a word or byte level comparison between different items of data.

73
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:22,200
So for example, if you have two big responses that are very similar and contain some minor

74
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:27,000
differences, you can send them to Burp Comparer and it will highlight exactly where the differences are.

75
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:35,240
Burp Extender is used to customise Burp's behaviour by loading Burp Extensions.

76
00:05:35,240 --> 00:05:39,400
So these can be extensions that you've written yourself using the Burp Extender API,

77
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:44,680
or they can be extensions from the BAP Store that have been written by other Burp users.

78
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:49,400
So there are hundreds of extensions here that have been contributed by the Burp user community.

79
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:53,080
These are used to enhance Burp's behaviour in all kinds of ways.

80
00:05:53,080 --> 00:05:54,600
So it's really worth playing with some of these.

81
00:05:54,600 --> 00:06:02,600
Under Project Options, we have numerous categories of option that affect how Burp will work in relation

82
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:03,560
to the current project.

83
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:06,920
So Project Options are saved in your project file.

84
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:10,680
If you're using a project file, you can save them in config files and load them again.

85
00:06:10,680 --> 00:06:12,600
We cover that in a separate tutorial.

86
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:17,800
But all of these settings are divided into separate areas, covering network connections,

87
00:06:18,440 --> 00:06:22,680
HTTP, TLS, session handling, and some other areas.

88
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:29,640
Under User Options are a different set of options that are more around your own installation of Burp

89
00:06:29,640 --> 00:06:33,960
and how you want it to behave, which aren't tied to particular projects.

90
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:36,120
So some interesting options here.

91
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:40,520
Under Display, this is where you can configure font size of your interface,

92
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:43,400
and whether to use a dark theme or a light theme.

93
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:47,400
So Burp will support both flavours of interface according to your preference.

94
00:06:48,280 --> 00:06:51,160
Under Miscellaneous, you can configure hotkeys.

95
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:55,560
So Burp supports tons of hotkeys for different common actions.

96
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:57,800
And this is where you can configure those.

97
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:02,360
There are many options that are hotkeyable, which don't have a hotkey assigned by default.

98
00:07:02,360 --> 00:07:05,000
And you can define one here if it's an action that you use a lot.

99
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:10,040
It's worth noting that there are some options which can be defined at the user level

100
00:07:10,040 --> 00:07:11,080
and at the project level.

101
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:17,400
So if you take, say, upstream proxy servers, if you're working on a penetration test for a particular

102
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:22,760
client engagement, you might be on their network and need to configure a certain proxy server.

103
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:29,880
You would do that under Project Options and configure that here by overriding the user options

104
00:07:29,880 --> 00:07:30,600
for this project.

105
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:35,800
Whereas if you have a proxy server on your home network that you use by default,

106
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:39,320
any time you're there, you can set it here in user options and that will be

107
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:41,400
the one that is used if you don't override it.

108
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:45,640
You can use the context menu within Burp everywhere.

109
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:51,320
Everywhere you see an entry in a table or any time you see an individual HTTP request,

110
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:54,920
you can use the context menu to access all kinds of actions.

111
00:07:54,920 --> 00:08:01,560
So you can send just this request to be scanned with a passive scan, active scan or a configurable scan.

112
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:06,600
You can send it to individual Burp tools to drive additional testing workflows.

113
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:08,440
You can view it in your browser.

114
00:08:08,440 --> 00:08:11,720
You can use various engagement tools for common purposes.

115
00:08:11,720 --> 00:08:17,960
So if you want to know other locations that link to a particular URL, particular request,

116
00:08:17,960 --> 00:08:19,560
you can use find references.

117
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:21,160
You can use content discovery.

118
00:08:21,720 --> 00:08:27,400
Webber will use big word lists to try to discover content beneath a URL that isn't necessarily linked.

119
00:08:27,400 --> 00:08:28,920
You can schedule tasks.

120
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:32,200
You can create a CSERF POC, do various other things.

121
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:37,880
In the menu bar at the top of the Burp UI, we have various other options.

122
00:08:37,880 --> 00:08:39,880
So on the Burp menu, we can do a search.

123
00:08:39,880 --> 00:08:45,720
This will search through all of the data in different Burp tools for items that match a particular expression.

124
00:08:46,440 --> 00:08:49,080
You can access Burp's configuration library.

125
00:08:49,080 --> 00:08:53,560
This is where you can maintain your own config files for different purposes,

126
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:57,240
or access various built-in configurations that come with Burp.

127
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:04,840
Under user options, you can restore defaults for all of your user options,

128
00:09:04,840 --> 00:09:09,480
or you can save your current user options to a config file or load it back later.

129
00:09:10,360 --> 00:09:14,600
Also on the Burp menu, we have various other tools that you will use occasionally.

130
00:09:14,600 --> 00:09:21,640
So Burp Infiltrator is an IAST tool that can be used to instrument an application that you plan to scan,

131
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:24,440
so that you can see inside the application as it's running,

132
00:09:24,440 --> 00:09:27,000
and get a lot more interesting information about it,

133
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:31,720
and find some dangerous code paths where your payloads might be hitting a vulnerability.

134
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:38,360
Burp ClickBandit is a tool for generating click-jacking attacks.

135
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:41,960
So the way this works is you copy the ClickBandit script into your browser,

136
00:09:41,960 --> 00:09:47,160
and you then walk through the sequence of actions that you want a victim to execute,

137
00:09:47,160 --> 00:09:51,000
and ClickBandit will generate a proof-of-concept click-jacking attack.

138
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:59,160
Burp Collaborator Client lets you work manually with Burp Collaborator payloads.

139
00:09:59,720 --> 00:10:03,400
You can use this to generate Burp Collaborator payloads, copy them to your clipboard,

140
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:07,640
you can then use them in your own attacks, sending requests through Burp Repeater,

141
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:10,760
or as payloads in Burp Intruder, or anything like that,

142
00:10:10,760 --> 00:10:16,120
and then you can poll for Collaborator interactions to see the results of using those payloads.

143
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:19,880
We'll cover Burp Collaborator and the Burp Collaborator Client in a separate tutorial.

144
00:10:22,040 --> 00:10:27,560
In the Project menu, you can also, as for user options, you can restore defaults for project

145
00:10:27,560 --> 00:10:33,720
options, you can save them to a config file or load them back, you can save a copy of your project,

146
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:37,960
so if you've started work in a temporary project, you decide you want to save it, you can save it to a

147
00:10:37,960 --> 00:10:42,760
project file, or if you're already working on a project file, you can save a copy. There are some

148
00:10:42,760 --> 00:10:48,200
menu options for the Intruder and Repeater tools, which we'll cover in separate tutorials. In the

149
00:10:48,200 --> 00:10:56,040
Window menu, you can detach individual Burp tools, float them out as a separate window if you want to

150
00:10:56,040 --> 00:11:03,320
arrange your UI that way. As well as detaching and reattaching individual tools, you can reorder the top

151
00:11:03,320 --> 00:11:10,040
level tabs if you prefer them to be in a different sequence. In the Help menu, you can access Burp's

152
00:11:10,040 --> 00:11:17,240
documentation, Getting Started help, you can go to the Support Center, you can see diagnostic information

153
00:11:17,240 --> 00:11:22,360
about your installation if you're experiencing any problems, if the embedded browser is not working

154
00:11:22,360 --> 00:11:28,520
correctly on your platform, you can run the health check to find out why, you can update your Burp license,

155
00:11:28,520 --> 00:11:35,560
check for updates, and other things. So that's a general guide to the Burp Suite user interface.

156
00:11:36,360 --> 00:11:42,440
Do check out our other video tutorials for more details on using each of the Burp Suite tools,

157
00:11:42,440 --> 00:11:45,240
features, and options that we've mentioned.

158
00:11:45,240 --> 00:11:47,240
!

159
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:51,080
Thank you!

160
00:11:51,080 --> 00:11:53,080
Thank you!

161
00:11:53,080 --> 00:11:58,920
Thank you!

162
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:00,920
Thank you!

163
00:12:00,920 --> 00:12:02,920
Thank you!

164
00:12:02,920 --> 00:12:04,920
Thank you!

165
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:06,920
Thank you!

166
00:12:06,920 --> 00:12:08,920
Thank you!

167
00:12:08,920 --> 00:12:10,920
Thank you!

168
00:12:10,920 --> 00:12:12,920
Thank you!

169
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:14,920
Thank you!

